Investigate adding a "door": a remote idea-intake endpoint so 'scheduler -i' style drops don't have to originate on mandark. Today ideas come in through mandark only -- confirmed 2026-07-25 during a senechal session: 'scheduler -i <project>' writes straight into that project's .claude/FOCUS.md on the local filesystem, so any drop from a phone/other host has no path in. Zach asked (senechal session, 2026-07-25) for this to be investigated, not built blind. Worth scoping: what the intake surface is (phone/web/email/SMS/git push to the bare remotes?), how it authenticates, and how a dropped note reaches mandark's FOCUS.md files -- ideally reusing the existing 'scheduler -i' insertion logic rather than a second writer. Note the security angle: an internet-reachable intake that can edit files on the laptop is a real attack surface, and senechal exists partly to guard secrets, so this needs a threat model before code.
